hi i'm mike coleman a developer advocate  here at google cloud and in this video i  want to talk to you about how you manage  your applications  after you've migrated them using migrate  for anthos  [Music]  getting your application containerized  is just the first step  you'll also need to consider how you  manage that application going forward  how do you handle base operating system  updates  application patching and other day two  tasks  the rest of this video aims to answer  some of those questions  as a quick side note i'm not going to  spend a lot of time talking about how  container images are constructed  if you're not familiar with that topic  pause the video  go check out the documentation on the  docker site and come back and rejoin me  also if you're not familiar with migrate  for anthos check out our earlier videos  where we provide an overview of the  service  as well as how to perform an actual  migration you can find a link to those  in the description below with that being  said  let's take a look at how migrant for  anthos creates the container image for a  migrated application  migrate for anthos automatically  examines a source vm and extracts the  workloads running there  into their own container images when  looking at the ongoing management of  those containers it's important to  understand  how their images are actually built the  container that you run in production is  a combination of two  intermediate images the first one is the  migrate for anthos runtime layer  and the other is what we refer to as the  non-runnable system layer  the non-runnable system layer contains  the extracted portion of your migrated  workloads  and will serve as the base for all  future updates  as the name implies this layer by itself  doesn't do anything  it's only by combining it with the  migrate for anthos runtime layer that  you get a container image  that you can deploy into production so  let's look at the docker files so you  can see how this works in practice  now the docker file uses a multi-stage  build to combine the two  artifacts together this first from  statement tells docker to use the  migrate for anthos 1.6.0 runtime image  from the google container registry  and to refer to it going forward as  quote unquote  migrate for anthos runtime the next from  tells the dockerfile to use the image  that was generated when you ran your  migration  it's also stored up in the google  container registry under your project  notice that docker will refer to this  image as quote unquote source content  as an aside while i am specifically  using google container registry in this  example  depending on where you're migrating from  we can also use aws's  elastic container registry or a local  registry  this section is where you'd run any  updates and we're going to cover that in  more detail in the next section  so for now we'll just skip over it  this line tells docker to copy the file  system starting at the root from the  migrate for anthos runtime  into the container image now remember  that the container image we're copying  into  is the source content image so  basically this command copies the  runtime container image  into the source content image and the  last line tells the container to run the  v2k.go script and start your migrated  application  now as i've said this docker file is  what's used to create the migrated  application configuration that you  initially deploy after running migrate  for anthos  but what do you do if down the road you  need to  update the image for some reason for  example you need to  install an updated dependency or patch  your application or  upgrade the underlying migrate for  anthos runtime  going back to our docker file now  updating the migrate for anthos runtime  is  actually pretty simple you just go back  to that first from line and you update  the version  if you need to update any part of your  application including system or language  libraries or the application code itself  you would do that after the second from  statement so for instance  if i'm a node developer i might copy in  a new package.json  and run npm install to update some of  the packages  my application is using or i could copy  in a new app.js file to update my actual  application code  the underlying file system in the  container is the same as what you had in  your vm  so you would just update the files in  their original locations  now from here you could manually run  docker build to create an  updated image push that to gcr and then  roll out the new version of your  application  but manually building the new container  images could become tedious  if you are regularly processing updates  a better approach is to use a tool like  cloud build  to automate the process with a modern  cicd pipeline  by leveraging a tool like cloud build  you can define triggers such that  whenever a change is pushed to your  source code repository  a new container image is created and  once that new image is created  you can perform manual or automated  tests  and then finally using either manual or  automated processes  you can push that code out into  production  let's take a look at how we would  implement a pipeline in practice  i'm going to construct a simple pipeline  that will connect to my github repo  such that when i push a new change to  github a new container image will be  pushed up to gcr  from there i'll update my kubernetes  yaml and roll that updated app into  production  okay so let's go ahead here we're going  to start in cloud build and i want to  show you the trigger that i built  earlier remember a trigger is something  that can kick off uh via different  processes so we have a  trigger here that when i push to a  branch in my github repo here to do  cloud build  um where i've installed the github app  for cloud build  when i hit the master branch with an  update it's going to go ahead  and run a docker file and it's going to  do that  in the root of the repository looking  for a file called dockerfile  and it's going to build us a new image  that is tagged with the short sha  of the build process right so that'll be  our unique tag  and then if we look down here below what  we can see is the actual docker file  built or the docker build command  where we're going to build that and  we're going to point to that docker file  that i just mentioned so that's what's  going to happen whenever i push a change  to my repo  now this is the docker file we talked  about this earlier in the video  you can see here is the run time here is  the non-runnable base that is created as  part of the migration process and then  what i've added to it  if we come down here is i'm basically  saying copy the  contents of this repo into user source  to  do in the container right in the  container image and that's where it  locate was located in the vm  that's where it was the code was located  in the container as well then they're  the same  so now what i want to do is i want to  come up and i want to actually go ahead  and make a change to my source code so  i'm going to come up here  into this show task  file up here at the top and i'm going to  click here and i'm going to go in  and i'm not running these on vms so it's  not a host it's actually a pod  so let me change this to pod and then i  will save this file out  and then i will move down here into the  terminal and i'll do a git add  star to add the change file i'll commit  that to the repo with a commit message  of use  update host string and then i'll go  ahead and do a push  now this push will kick off that trigger  that we just talked about so if i come  into history  you can see it's already building so  we'll come in here  and i'll show you here a second when  this comes up you can actually see  there's that commit message i  i used update host string this is gonna  take a bit so let me pause the video and  i'll rejoin you when it's done  okay so we've finished up here and if we  come down we can see  uh there's where we copied the source  code into the image as i mentioned  and here's that short sha and we're  going to use that  to do the update to our application so  let's go to the image repository here  container registry look at the images  and  that's where the non-runnable base gets  stored by the migration process  and there's the to do directory that is  where we actually get our deployable  applications so there's the one we just  created  and now we're going to deploy that by  opening up a cloud shell  and editing the kubernetes yaml file so  let's open this up  and we're going to move into the  directory where the artifacts are stored  and we're going to edit the deployment  spec.yaml  and we're just going to scroll down here  and we're going to change that  tag on the image name to the one we just  created  we're going to save that out and then  we're going to go into the terminal  and we're going to go ahead and well  before we do that let me point this out  so it says serve by host that's what  we're going to change  so now let me go here and i want to go  ahead and  um clear the screen and then we'll do a  coupe ctl apply  minus f deployment spec.yaml and that's  going to update  the stateful set with a new image let's  do a get pods with a watch so we can  watch that happen  and while that's happening i want to  just talk about a couple of things  one this is not exactly what you would  do right there would be a step between  building the image  and deploying it and that would involve  some sort of testing right we talked  about that and the other thing  is um you could automate this step if  you wanted to right  in the video we don't really have time  to go through that process but  um you could fully automate this so you  automate your testing you automate the  uh sign-offs and the release and then  you automate the push to production  uh here we're just basically automating  the building of the container image  we're assuming a manual  uh test step and a manual deployment  step all right so we're almost done here  the container should be up and running  in just a couple seconds  and looks like it's just about done so  let's go ahead and check on that  application and see if our change got  reflected so we'll come up here click on  to do list  app and you can see down here actually  there it is it's now served by a pod  instead of a host so the application has  been updated so in this video  we talked about how you can use a cloud  build build trigger uh connected to a  github repo  to automate the creation or the updating  of your migrated  applications that about wraps it up for  this video i hope you found it useful  if you want more information on  migrateforanthos we have some additional  videos posted here on youtube so check  those out  and then head on over to cloud.google  migrate anthos to learn more  you
 

